  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSurface low pressure over Saskatchewan will gradually churn\neastward through the day today. Currently, associated surface \ntrough is producing a line of light rain moving west to east \nmainly over central North Dakota. While warmer air is \naccompanying this system, current sub-freezing temperatures in \nparts of the south central, especially the southern James River \nValley of ND, could result in brief light freezing rain. That \nsaid, upstream obs have been showing temperatures warming four \nto six degrees before and at the onset of rainfall. If this \ntrend continues, then freezing rain would become unlikely within\nthe forecast area. Once this system passes through, expect \nsunny skies and breezy westerly winds this afternoon, along with\nabove average highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s.\n\nWith a tightening pressure gradient, stronger winds are possible\nin the far southwest later this morning and early afternoon.\nWhile they could briefly touch advisory criteria, believe the\noverall expectation is that they will remain sub-advisory over \nmost locations, most of the time. For Saturday, a cold frontal \nboundary will pass through thus shifting winds to the northwest \nand bringing windy conditions across most of the state. Once \nagain, the strongest winds are expected to be over parts of \nsouthwestern North Dakota. While the EFI hasn't been terribly \nexcited about winds on Saturday, the BUFKIT momentum transfer \ntool brings solid advisory winds to parts of the southwest for a\nfew hours using both RAP and HRRR forecast soundings. \n\nIn addition to windy conditions, the passing fropa Saturday\ncould bring a mix of light rain and snow to parts of the \nforecast area during the day. Overall, hi-res models indicate \nstreaky isolated to scattered returns, so wouldn't expect \nwidespread areal coverage. Either way, little to no snow \naccumulations are expected.\n\nBeyond today's continued above average temperatures, a cooling\ntrend is on tap through this weekend. By Sunday, forecast highs\nare in the mid 20s to mid 30s, with lows in the teens to mid \n20s Sunday night. Overall seasonable to seasonably warm \ntemperatures are then forecast for the next workweek with highs \nmostly in the mid 30s and low 40s, and lows mostly in the 20s.",
